Work Summary

Implemented Section 25 of the MW–M31 analysis pipeline, focusing on deriving
stellar mass and star-formation proxies from a purely collisionless N-body
simulation. Since no gas physics or explicit star formation is present, this
section constructs physically motivated *surrogate diagnostics* from dark
matter structure, kinematics, and density fields.

The goal is to reconstruct where star formation *would occur* in a full
hydrodynamic counterpart simulation using density thresholds, dynamical
instability criteria, and structural evolution of the merger remnant.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Key Physics Implemented

- High-density thresholding (ρ > ρ_SF):
  Identifies star-formation–eligible regions using local volumetric density
  as a proxy for Schmidt–Kennicutt star formation triggering.

- Toomre stability analysis:
  Computed Q(R,t) using surface density, radial velocity dispersion, and
  epicyclic frequency approximation.
  → Q < 1 identifies gravitational instability regions.

- Sérsic profile fitting:
  Fit Σ(R) at multiple epochs to track structural evolution of the remnant.
  Extracted:
    • Sérsic index n(t)
    • Effective radius R_e(t)
    • Fit quality χ²

- Star-forming mass fraction:
  Defined as fraction of inner mass (r < 30 kpc) exceeding density threshold.

- Star-forming radius:
  Radius enclosing 90% of SF-eligible mass, tracking spatial contraction/
  expansion of active regions.

- Cumulative SF proxy:
  Time-integrated M_SF(t) used as a proxy for stellar mass assembly history.

